tlement  goes up in accordance with the cost of  living that you could be quite  significantly worse off and that will  need looking at quite closely although  he has put in this inflation proofing of  1.8% Ian that looks pretty generous  compared with the current rate of  inflation but speaking to the pensions  regulator there is a chance that as I  said if you're a young  a pensioner here and that you could be  drawing down on your pension for a  considerable number of years the gap  between what you might have expected in  the present deal and the gap with what  you might have had under BHS could be  significant now I hope that's not the  case but I think it's a risk to be  identified and mitigated and certainly  Anil Frank's committee will want to look  at this - well Frank let's bring you in  at this point I mean you've said that  this isn't there are not issues which  are not sold by this which we will  obviously be looking at and the course  and everyone else will be what do you  mean by that well I mean that this is as  Ian saying a milestone an important one  but pensioners won't get the pension  increase they thought they would get but  they will all be starting at the pension  rate that they thought they and they  believed and they have actually paid in  full  that's a very I mean that's a totally  different situation to what we had when  we had to fill it before the committee  it's a success for the pension regulator  she hasn't dealt in these big figures  before and it's as a success there but  the Prime Minister before she thinks  about any knighted or anything like that  knows that there's four major reports to  come in and an Ian was touching on those  I mean that the revenue is looking into  Dominic Chapelle I think I believe  actually a writ has arrested him and  obviously released him but they're  serious charges they're looking out  there there's the Serious Fraud Office  there's the liquidator and so on and at  the end of the day we'll have a fuller  picture about what went wrong and who  dragged down BHS take what you've met  fall sorry Frank you've mentioned  Dominic chappelle's position there now  of course the the regulator has now  dropped all enforcement action against  Sir Philip  she still has enforcement action in  place against Dominic Chapelle oh and do  you think the authorities have gone far  enough yet to bring Dominic Chapelle  Tabuk no and I think by bringing him to  book whether it's through the regulator  and thereby through the courts or it's  by the revenue through the courts then  in fact we will have we might begin to  see or understand  much more of this story but the I know  Ian's committee is taking this forward  in that the the Prime Minister is really  keen on what are the lessons for  corporate government's the running of  firms the way people are treated their  pension entitlements and so on and both  Ian and I have written to the Prime  Minister to say here's a report we've  done the lessons we've drawn for private  companies the governance of large  private companies who are often we now  discover more important than many many  many public companies we're hoping that  she's going to chair a meeting at  Downing Street with the really good  examples of those who run private  companies thinking about their workers  their pensioners as well as their  shareholders and consumers and to start  to lay down rules so that other parts of  civic greens Empire like Arcadia and  other companies where there are problems  will have to be enforced to adopt good  pack the best practice okay well we'll  be discussing Arkady later in the  program but it writes we have the  pensions green paper last week it looked  like the government was backing down  from giving the Pensions Regulator real  teeth to block mergers and acquisitions  do you believe that's a mistake Frank  would know more about the pensions green  paper than I do chairing the work and  pensions committee in terms of green  papers and the Prime Minister lodged a  couple of weeks ago the corporate  governance green paper where she'd been  previously talking about workers on  boards and other things to improve the  decision-making process of companies in  our country she seems to have backed  down significantly on large parts of  that and Frank's absolutely right in  terms of many private companies that are  not listed on the stock exchange they  are massive they're massive employees  employers and have large numbers of  pensions as well and they shouldn't be  able to escape any sort of regulatory  environment that tries to protect not  just shareholders but pensioners and  employees too so you think the Prime  Minister's been nobbled by the interests  of big business then in I think you'll  have to ask the Prime Minister when you  interview her next Ian but what we want  to do on the on the base Select  Committee is look at proper governance  we're coming to the end  our inquiry now we've been looking  things like executive pay on the  responsibilities and duties of directors  because Frank's absolutely right when we  looked into BHS and the manner in which  it was sold the level of corporate  governance was absolutely appalling the  chair of the parent company wasn't even  at present at the meeting when it was  decided to sell BHS it was all a fiefdom  designed to help Sir Philip green rather  than think about the pensioners and the  employees and if we'd had proper  stringent corporate governance reporting  requirements in place perhaps this sorry  tail might not have happened does that  mean you think some of the other BHS  directors the the likes of Karren Brady  people who have places on freely green  sports they still have a have questions  to answer them there's an important part  of the Companies Act legislation it's  called section 172 and it deals with  directors duties that in terms of  directors have to think about wider  stakeholders and not just the  shareholders I think it's very